"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"][8]={"school_physics_en:M8-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M8-L1 – Forms of energy and energy transfer<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M8 – Energy, work and power<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>A bicycle dynamo powers a lamp. The wheel becomes a little harder to\nturn as the lamp lights up and warms slightly. Where does the lamp’s\nenergy come from, and why is it wrong to describe the process as\n“creating energy from nothing”?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical concepts and model<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Energy<\/strong> is a conserved physical quantity. It can be\nassigned to a system, described using various forms and transferred\nbetween systems. The SI unit of energy is the <strong>joule<\/strong>\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{J}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>).\nSchool models distinguish, for instance, kinetic energy, the\ngravitational potential energy of the Earth–object system, elastic\nenergy, internal or thermal energy, chemical energy and electrical\nenergy. These are not separate substances: they are different ways of\ntracking one conserved quantity.<\/p>\n<p>An <strong>energy conversion<\/strong> is a change in the form of\nenergy within a chosen system. An <strong>energy transfer<\/strong>\ncrosses the boundary of that system, for example via mechanical work,\nelectrical work, heating or radiation. State the boundary explicitly. In\nthe cycling example chemical energy from food is transferred by muscular\nwork. The bicycle rotates, the dynamo transfers electrical energy to the\nlamp, and the lamp transfers energy to its surroundings by light and\nheating.<\/p>\n<p>In an <strong>energy-flow diagram<\/strong>, boxes identify systems or\nenergy accounts. Arrows describe transfers, while branches describe\ndifferent outgoing routes. An arrow does not imply that energy is a\nmaterial fluid. <strong>Original diagram:<\/strong> Bicycle–dynamo–lamp\ntransfer network, branching into light and heating.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_044_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Diagram: cyclist transfers mechanical energy to a dynamo, then electrically to a lamp which emits light and heats surroundings.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Diagram: cyclist transfers mechanical\nenergy to a dynamo, then electrically to a lamp which emits light and\nheats surroundings.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Energy forms and transfers in a bicycle dynamo model.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example or application<\/h2>\n<p>A small device receives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>120<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">120\\,\\mathrm{J}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof electrical energy during a ten-second test. It transfers\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>24<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">24\\,\\mathrm{J}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nas light. In a simplified account the rest is assigned to heating and\nother outgoing pathways:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>120<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>24<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>96<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_{\\mathrm{other\\,outputs}}=120\\,\\mathrm{J}-24\\,\\mathrm{J}=96\\,\\mathrm{J}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The input energy has not vanished. The <strong>intended<\/strong>\noutput (light) is only part of the total transfer.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe observation and experiment<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Safe observation:<\/strong> Use a suitable LED and an\napproved, current-limited extra-low-voltage classroom supply to observe\nlight emission and any gentle warming. Do not touch exposed electrical\ncontacts; never experiment with mains wiring or dismantled batteries.\nAlternatively draw the complete energy path of a bicycle dynamo on\npaper.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Name three energy forms and one system for each.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does energy conversion differ from energy transfer?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A device receives 150 J and emits 40 J as light. Account for the\nremainder.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does an energy-flow diagram need a system boundary?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For example kinetic energy of a bicycle, chemical energy associated\nwith food, and internal energy of a warm body.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Conversion changes the form in the selected system; transfer crosses\na system boundary.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">150 J − 40 J = 110 J transferred through other pathways.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It defines which changes occur inside and which transfers cross the\nboundary.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and transition<\/h2>\n<p>The naming of energy forms depends partly on the chosen model. The\nexample numbers are invented teaching data. Light and heating are\noutgoing transfer mechanisms; “heat” is not treated as a permanent\nsubstance stored inside an object.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Next:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M8-L2<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/rlp-online/c-faecher/physik/themen-und-inhalte\">Berlin-Brandenburg\nEducation Server – Physics curriculum topics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Mechanical energy and\nwork; energy transformation · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/rlp-online/c-faecher/physik/kompetenzen-und-standards\">Berlin-Brandenburg\nEducation Server – Physics competences<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Conservation, conversion\nand efficiency in competence standards · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 ·\nContent verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/mechanik/arbeit-energie-und-leistung\">LEIFIphysik\n– Work, energy and power<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Forms, conversion, work, power and\nconservation of energy · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/bildungsministerkonferenz/bildungsthemen/bildungsstandards.html\">KMK\n– Physics standards for intermediate school certificate (2024)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nOfficial index of 2024 physics competence standards · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M8-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M8-L2 – Kinetic and gravitational potential energy<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M8 – Energy, work and power<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>A quickly rolling ball can move an obstacle; the same ball may fall\nfrom a shelf. Both states can be described using energy, but different\nmeasured quantities matter: speed in one case and height in the\nother.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical concepts and model<\/h2>\n<p>For an object of mass\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmoving at speed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>v<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe nonrelativistic school model gives its <strong>kinetic\nenergy<\/strong>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mi>m<\/mi><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_{\\mathrm{kin}}=\\frac12 mv^2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Doubling speed at fixed mass <strong>quadruples<\/strong> kinetic\nenergy. Speed therefore has a particularly strong influence on energy.\nThis formula describes centre-of-mass motion; a rolling object may have\nadditional rotational kinetic energy.<\/p>\n<p>Near Earth’s surface we write the <strong>gravitational potential\nenergy<\/strong>, measured relative to a chosen zero level, as<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>g<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>9.81<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_{\\mathrm{pot}}=mgh,\\qquad g\\approx9.81\\,\\mathrm{N/kg}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Here\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis height above the reference level. Strictly the energy belongs to the\n<strong>Earth–object system<\/strong>. We are free to select another\nreference level. Changes in gravitational energy, rather than the\narbitrary zero, determine physical predictions. Energy is measured in\njoules:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{J}=1\\,\\mathrm{N\\,m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nConvert every measurement to SI units before calculating.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Original diagram:<\/strong> Two matching carts with speeds\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>v<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>v<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand a raised mass above a reference height.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_045_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"At equal mass doubling speed quadruples kinetic energy; raising a mass increases energy relative to the chosen reference.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">At equal mass doubling speed quadruples\nkinetic energy; raising a mass increases energy relative to the chosen\nreference.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Comparing kinetic and gravitational potential energy.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example or application<\/h2>\n<p>A body of mass\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=2.0\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntravels at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=3.0\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIts kinetic energy is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3.0<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_{\\mathrm{kin}}=\\tfrac12\\cdot2.0\\cdot(3.0)^2\\,\\mathrm{J}=9.0\\,\\mathrm{J}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>If the same body is lifted <strong>from rest<\/strong> by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=1.5\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nits gravitational energy increases by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>9.81<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>29.43<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta E_{\\mathrm{pot}}=2.0\\cdot9.81\\cdot1.5\\,\\mathrm{J}=29.43\\,\\mathrm{J}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThese are <strong>two different situations<\/strong> and should not be\nsummed without a defined common state.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe observation and experiment<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Safe comparison:<\/strong> Let a marble roll down a fixed low\nramp from two starting heights. Observe its motion on a flat run-out\nsection. Keep small objects away from young children and use low heights\nonly. Because rotation and friction are present,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mi>m<\/mi><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">mgh=\\tfrac12mv^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nalone does not completely describe the experiment.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What happens to kinetic energy when speed doubles?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_{\\mathrm{kin}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=0.50\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=4.0\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the increase in gravitational energy for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3.0\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nraised by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.0\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does the reference height matter?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It becomes four times as large because speed is squared.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.50<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>4<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac12\\cdot0.50\\cdot4^2=4.0\\,\\mathrm{J}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.0<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>9.81<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>58.86<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3.0\\cdot9.81\\cdot2.0=58.86\\,\\mathrm{J}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The absolute number depends on the chosen reference; the difference\nbetween two heights does not.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and transition<\/h2>\n<p>The classical kinetic formula fails at very high speeds. A constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>g<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis not adequate over large altitude ranges. Rolling objects carry\nrotational energy, and real tracks also transfer energy by friction,\nheating and sound.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Next:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M8-L3<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/mechanik/arbeit-energie-und-leistung/grundwissen/kinetische-energie\">LEIFIphysik\n– Kinetic energy<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Squared-speed dependence and the SI joule · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/mechanik/arbeit-energie-und-leistung\">LEIFIphysik\n– Work, energy and power<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Forms, conversion, work, power and\nconservation of energy · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/rlp-online/c-faecher/physik/themen-und-inhalte\">Berlin-Brandenburg\nEducation Server – Physics curriculum topics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Mechanical energy and\nwork; energy transformation · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/bildungsministerkonferenz/bildungsthemen/bildungsstandards.html\">KMK\n– Physics standards for intermediate school certificate (2024)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nOfficial index of 2024 physics competence standards · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M8-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M8-L3 – Conservation and conversion of energy<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M8 – Energy, work and power<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>A cart rolls down a track and becomes faster at the bottom. How can\ngravitational energy decrease without violating energy conservation? A\ncomplete account includes the cart and its surroundings.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical concepts and model<\/h2>\n<p>The <strong>conservation of energy<\/strong> states that the\n<strong>total energy<\/strong> of a completely isolated system remains\nconstant. Energy may change form or cross a chosen system boundary, but\ncannot appear from nowhere or cease to exist. A valid energy account\ntherefore includes all relevant stores and transfers.<\/p>\n<p>For an ideal body sliding along a track without rotation or\nfriction,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_{\\mathrm{kin}}+E_{\\mathrm{pot}}=\\mathrm{constant}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>is a useful approximation. It is <strong>not<\/strong> a universal\nconservation law involving only these two mechanical terms. Along a real\ntrack, energy passes to the cart, track and environment and increases\ninternal energy. With a suitably broad system boundary, we may describe\nthe result as<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">,<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">,<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">,<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">,<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_{\\mathrm{pot,initial}}+E_{\\mathrm{kin,initial}}=E_{\\mathrm{pot,final}}+E_{\\mathrm{kin,final}}+E_{\\mathrm{thermal}}+E_{\\mathrm{other}}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The last two terms stand for other properly accounted-for energy\ncomponents; they are not sources of “lost” energy. When the system is\nnarrower, transfers beyond its boundary must be listed separately.\n<strong>Original diagram:<\/strong> Bar chart of a cart: initially 100 J\ngravitational energy; finally 70 J kinetic energy and 30 J in other\naccounts.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_046_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"100 J of initial gravitational energy become 70 J kinetic energy and 30 J in other accounts.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">100 J of initial gravitational energy\nbecome 70 J kinetic energy and 30 J in other accounts.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>All energy components belong in the total balance.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example or application<\/h2>\n<p>A cart loses\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">100\\,\\mathrm{J}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof gravitational energy relative to a defined height. Initially it is at\nrest; at the bottom it has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>70<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">70\\,\\mathrm{J}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof kinetic energy. With no further changes the remaining accounts must\ncontain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>30<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">30\\,\\mathrm{J}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>70<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>30<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">100\\,\\mathrm{J}=70\\,\\mathrm{J}+30\\,\\mathrm{J}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Some energy warms the wheels and track; a smaller part may travel\naway as sound. Without measurements, we must <strong>not<\/strong> label\nthe entire 30 J as heat stored in a single object.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe observation and experiment<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Safe observation:<\/strong> Watch a toy marble move along a\nshort, low wooden track. Compare its starting and final height during a\nback-and-forth motion. Fix the track securely, shield the run-out area\nand keep fingers away. Begin with a qualitative energy account; do not\ninfer exact frictional energy from observation alone.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">State conservation of energy for an isolated system.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A ball begins with 80 J of gravitational energy and has 65 J kinetic\nenergy at the bottom. Account for the rest.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_{\\mathrm{kin}}+E_{\\mathrm{pot}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnot generally constant in the presence of friction?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What changes in the account if the track lies outside the chosen\nsystem?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The sum of all energy components remains constant.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">80 J − 65 J = 15 J for other energy components or outward\ntransfers.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Mechanical energy can be transformed into internal energy and\ntransferred to other objects.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Energy transferred to the track must be recorded as a transfer\nacross the chosen boundary.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and transition<\/h2>\n<p>The bar-chart numbers are fictional teaching values. In an open\nsystem the stored energy need not be constant when energy enters or\nleaves. Measurement uncertainty may explain small apparent imbalances;\nthose do not establish a failure of conservation.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Next:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M8-L4<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/mechanik/arbeit-energie-und-leistung/grundwissen/energieerhaltung\">LEIFIphysik\n– Energy conservation<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Ideal conservation of mechanical energy and\nfrictional pathways · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/mechanik/arbeit-energie-und-leistung\">LEIFIphysik\n– Work, energy and power<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Forms, conversion, work, power and\nconservation of energy · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/rlp-online/c-faecher/physik/themen-und-inhalte\">Berlin-Brandenburg\nEducation Server – Physics curriculum topics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Mechanical energy and\nwork; energy transformation · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/bildungsministerkonferenz/bildungsthemen/bildungsstandards.html\">KMK\n– Physics standards for intermediate school certificate (2024)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nOfficial index of 2024 physics competence standards · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M8-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M8-L4 – Mechanical work and power<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M8 – Energy, work and power<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>Two people lift the same box through the same height. One takes five\nseconds; the other takes ten. The gravitational energy gain can be\nidentical while the faster lift requires more power.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical concepts and model<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Mechanical work<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>W<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis an energy transfer involving a force whose point of application\nmoves. For a <strong>constant<\/strong> force and straight\ndisplacement,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>W<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W=Fs\\cos\\alpha,<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>where\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>α<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the angle between force and displacement. With force along the motion\nthis reduces to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>W<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W=Fs<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA force opposite the displacement does negative work. A force\nperpendicular to displacement does <strong>zero<\/strong> work in this\nmodel. The unit is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{J}=1\\,\\mathrm{N\\,m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>Work is <strong>not stored inside a body<\/strong>; it describes an\nenergy transfer that can change a system’s energy. Lifting a box slowly\nincreases gravitational energy by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta E_{\\mathrm{pot}}=mgh<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe lifting force does approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+mgh<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof work, while gravity does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-mgh<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Average power<\/strong> is energy transferred per unit\ntime:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>P<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">¯<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>W<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\overline P=\\frac{W}{\\Delta t}=\\frac{\\Delta E}{\\Delta t}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>When comparing the magnitudes of useful transfers, use the\ncorresponding positive quantity; general signs depend on the accounting\nconvention.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{W}=1\\,\\mathrm{J/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDo not confuse <strong>watt<\/strong>, a unit, with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>W<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe symbol used for work. <strong>Original diagram:<\/strong> Equal\nlifting work completed in two different time intervals.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_047_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Same mass is lifted through the same height; halving the duration doubles average mechanical power.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Same mass is lifted through the same\nheight; halving the duration doubles average mechanical\npower.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>The same work can require different power.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example or application<\/h2>\n<p>A box of mass\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=10\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis raised by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=1.5\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat approximately constant speed. The energy transferred by the lifting\nforce is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>9.81<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>147.15<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W_{\\mathrm{lift}}=mgh=10\\cdot9.81\\cdot1.5\\,\\mathrm{J}=147.15\\,\\mathrm{J}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Over\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5.0\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe average mechanical lifting power is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>147.15<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>5.0<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>29.43<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">147.15/5.0=29.43\\,\\mathrm{W}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nOver\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10.0\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nit is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>14.715<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">14.715\\,\\mathrm{W}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis is <strong>useful mechanical lifting power<\/strong>, not the\nperson’s total metabolic power.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe observation and experiment<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Safe measurement:<\/strong> Raise a light, unbreakable object\nof roughly 0.5 kg by 0.5 m and time the movement. Keep the floor clear,\ndo not lift above head height and protect feet from dropped objects.\nRepeat using a different lifting duration without adding heavy weights.\nTreat the result as an approximation to average mechanical lifting\npower.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Besides constant force, what condition makes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>W<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W=Fs<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nvalid?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the work of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">20\\,\\mathrm{N}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nacting through\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3.0\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the same direction.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the average power for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>180<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">180\\,\\mathrm{J}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntransferred in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6.0\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What work does a force perpendicular to displacement perform?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Force and displacement must point along the same line and direction\n(otherwise use the parallel component).<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>20<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>3.0<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>60<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">20\\cdot3.0=60\\,\\mathrm{J}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>180<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>6.0<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>30<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">180/6.0=30\\,\\mathrm{W}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Zero, because\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msup><mn>90<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos90^\\circ=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Actual muscular work and metabolic power involve additional internal\nprocesses. Varying forces require an integral rather than a single\narbitrary value of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Fs<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf an object accelerates while lifting, the gravitational energy\nincrease alone is not all the work.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Next:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M8-L5<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/mechanik/energieerhaltung-und-umwandlung/grundwissen/arbeit-als-energietransfer\">LEIFIphysik\n– Work as energy transfer<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Work as energy transfer and force\ncomponent along displacement · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/mechanik/arbeit-energie-und-leistung/aufgabe/definition-der-leistung\">LEIFIphysik\n– Definition of power<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Average power as energy change per interval\n· Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/rlp-online/c-faecher/physik/themen-und-inhalte\">Berlin-Brandenburg\nEducation Server – Physics curriculum topics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Mechanical energy and\nwork; energy transformation · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/bildungsministerkonferenz/bildungsthemen/bildungsstandards.html\">KMK\n– Physics standards for intermediate school certificate (2024)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nOfficial index of 2024 physics competence standards · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M8-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M8-L5 – Efficiency and degradation of energy usefulness<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M8 – Energy, work and power<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>A lamp converts electrical energy and emits light. Not all supplied\njoules become visible light: some energy is transferred through other\nradiation or by warming surrounding objects. Does the rest of the energy\ndisappear?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical concepts and model<\/h2>\n<p>The <strong>efficiency<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>η<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncompares energy <strong>useful for a defined purpose<\/strong> with the\n<strong>energy supplied<\/strong> over the same interval:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>η<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">or over equal times<\/mtext><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>η<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta=\\frac{E_{\\mathrm{useful}}}{E_{\\mathrm{input}}}\\qquad\\text{or over equal times}\\qquad\\eta=\\frac{P_{\\mathrm{useful}}}{P_{\\mathrm{input}}}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Efficiency has no unit and is often expressed as a percentage. For a\ncorrectly bounded passive converter\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>η<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\leq\\eta\\leq1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n“Useful” is <strong>purpose-dependent<\/strong>. Heat output is desirable\nfrom a room heater, whereas the intended output of an electric motor is\nnormally mechanical motion. A resistive heater may transfer almost all\nof its input energy into the room as heat while being unsuitable for\nproducing motion.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Degradation of energy usefulness<\/strong> means that energy\nis still conserved but, after spreading through an environment, is\n<strong>less available for a directed later purpose<\/strong>. A slightly\nwarmer room cannot simply provide all the electrical energy previously\nused. Saying energy is “lost” generally means <strong>lost from the\nuseful account<\/strong>, not destroyed.<\/p>\n<p>For two distinct conversion stages, efficiencies multiply:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>η<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>η<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta_{\\mathrm{total}}=\\eta_1\\eta_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nprovided the useful output of the first stage feeds the second.\n<strong>Original diagram:<\/strong> A 200 J input dividing into 150 J\nuseful energy and 50 J other transfers, with proportional widths.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_048_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"200 J input is accounted for as 150 J useful output and 50 J other transfer; efficiency 75 percent.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">200 J input is accounted for as 150 J\nuseful output and 50 J other transfer; efficiency 75\npercent.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Useful output is only part of conserved total energy.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example or application<\/h2>\n<p>A model motor receives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>200<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_{\\mathrm{input}}=200\\,\\mathrm{J}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof electrical energy and delivers\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>150<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_{\\mathrm{useful}}=150\\,\\mathrm{J}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmechanically. Therefore<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>η<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>150<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mn>200<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.75<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>75<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta=\\frac{150\\,\\mathrm{J}}{200\\,\\mathrm{J}}=0.75=75\\,\\%.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The other\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">50\\,\\mathrm{J}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis accounted for by heating and other pathways. With a second stage of\nefficiency\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.80<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta_2=0.80<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe useful final output is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>150<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.80<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>120<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">150\\cdot0.80=120\\,\\mathrm{J}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe overall efficiency is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.75<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.80<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.60<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>60<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.75\\cdot0.80=0.60=60\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe observation and experiment<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Safe modelling exercise:<\/strong> Use 20 paper tokens to\nrepresent the input, putting 15 tokens in a useful-output pile and five\nin an other-outputs pile. Calculate efficiency without working with\ndangerous apparatus. Measure electrical efficiency only with approved,\nsupervised classroom equipment.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>η<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor 36 J of useful output from a 60 J input.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does 60% efficiency not mean that 40% of energy disappears?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Two stages have\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.80<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta_1=0.80<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.50<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta_2=0.50<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFind the overall efficiency.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is the term “useful energy” purpose-dependent?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>η<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>36<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>60<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.60<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>60<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta=36/60=0.60=60\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The remainder is transferred along other routes, and conservation\nstill holds.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.80<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.50<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.40<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.80\\cdot0.50=0.40=40\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For instance, heating is useful for a heater but usually not the\ndesired output of a mechanical motor.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Efficiency requires specified system boundaries and matching\nintervals. A heat pump can have a coefficient of performance above 1\nwithout violating conservation; this coefficient is not an ordinary\nenergy-conversion efficiency because environmental heat is transferred\nas well. Energy degradation is treated qualitatively here, without a\nfull entropy model.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Next:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M8-L6<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/mechanik/arbeit-energie-und-leistung/grundwissen/wirkungsgrad\">LEIFIphysik\n– Efficiency<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Efficiency based on energy or power and successive\nconversion stages · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/mechanik/arbeit-energie-und-leistung/grundwissen/energieerhaltung\">LEIFIphysik\n– Energy conservation<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Ideal conservation of mechanical energy and\nfrictional pathways · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/rlp-online/c-faecher/physik/kompetenzen-und-standards\">Berlin-Brandenburg\nEducation Server – Physics competences<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Conservation, conversion\nand efficiency in competence standards · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 ·\nContent verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/bildungsministerkonferenz/bildungsthemen/bildungsstandards.html\">KMK\n– Physics standards for intermediate school certificate (2024)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nOfficial index of 2024 physics competence standards · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M8-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M8-L6 – Energy storage and sustainable supply<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M8 – Energy, work and power<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>A battery can run a small lamp for a long time, while another device\ncan deliver very high power only briefly. Asking “How big is the store?”\nis insufficient to decide whether it serves a particular purpose.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical concepts and model<\/h2>\n<p>An <strong>energy store<\/strong> can provide energy later by\nmaintaining a particular state, such as chemical energy in a\nrechargeable battery, gravitational energy of water in an upper\nreservoir or internal energy in a heat store. Electrical energy is often\nconverted into other forms for storage and later converted back into\nelectricity.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Capacity<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>E<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis an amount of energy measured in J, Wh or kWh. <strong>Power<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>P<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndescribes how quickly energy can be delivered, measured in W or kW. For\nconstant output power and idealised loss-free use,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>E<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=\\frac{E}{P},\\qquad 1\\,\\mathrm{kWh}=3.6\\,\\mathrm{MJ}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>A device with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm{kWh}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof <em>usable<\/em> energy can ideally deliver\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{kW}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor <strong>two hours<\/strong>, not two kilowatts of energy. In reality,\nstate of charge, temperature, converters, discharge rate and ageing\nlimit performance. <strong>Round-trip efficiency<\/strong> compares\nuseful energy recovered after a charge–discharge cycle to the energy\nsupplied while charging.<\/p>\n<p>Sustainable supply requires a systems view: electricity generation,\nstores, grids, raw materials, land use, reuse and environmental impacts\ninteract. A battery often serves a different role from pumped hydro or a\nthermal store. <strong>Original diagram:<\/strong> Three storage\nprinciples labelled with their energy form and a small\ncapacity-versus-power comparison.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_049_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Comparison of battery, pumped storage and thermal store; example shows duration from usable capacity and power.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Comparison of battery, pumped storage and\nthermal store; example shows duration from usable capacity and\npower.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Capacity and power describe different aspects of a\nstore.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example or application<\/h2>\n<p>A hypothetical store provides\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E=3.0\\,\\mathrm{kWh}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof <strong>usable<\/strong> energy. At a constant electrical load\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.75<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P=0.75\\,\\mathrm{kW}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe ideal duration is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><mrow><mn>0.75<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=\\frac{3.0\\,\\mathrm{kWh}}{0.75\\,\\mathrm{kW}}=4.0\\,\\mathrm{h}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>This is an energy account assuming constant power. If a charging\ncycle consumes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4.0\\,\\mathrm{kWh}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand then delivers\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3.0\\,\\mathrm{kWh}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof useful output, round-trip efficiency is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.75<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3/4=0.75<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nor 75%. The figures are teaching assumptions, not typical manufacturer\nspecifications.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe observation and experiment<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Safe modelling task:<\/strong> Compare two fictional storage\ncards with capacities in Wh and rated outputs in W. Calculate which\nstore can power a stated device for longer. Never open or damage battery\ncells, attempt improvised short-circuit demonstrations or connect\nexperimental devices to household mains power.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Explain the difference between 2 kWh and 2 kW.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">An ideal store provides 1.2 kWh at a constant 300 W. For how\nlong?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A store takes 5 kWh to charge and returns 4 kWh. Find round-trip\nefficiency.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Name two selection criteria besides energy capacity.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">2 kWh is an amount of energy; 2 kW is a rate of energy transfer, or\npower.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.300<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.2\\,\\mathrm{kWh}/0.300\\,\\mathrm{kW}=4\\,\\mathrm{h}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>5<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.80<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>80<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4/5=0.80=80\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For example output power, charging time, lifetime, materials,\nsafety, efficiency or environmental effects.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and transition<\/h2>\n<p>The values in the example are fictional and are not up-to-date\nperformance specifications. Different storage technologies have\ndifferent physical forms and real-world constraints; there is no\nuniversally appropriate ranking. Sustainability requires considering\nmanufacture and disposal as well as use.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Next:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M8-L7<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.umweltbundesamt.de/themen/klima-energie/energieversorgung/stromspeicher\">German\nEnvironment Agency – Electricity storage<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Storage principles,\ncapacity and different conversion routes · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 ·\nContent verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/rlp-online/c-faecher/physik/themen-und-inhalte\">Berlin-Brandenburg\nEducation Server – Physics curriculum topics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Mechanical energy and\nwork; energy transformation · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/mechanik/arbeit-energie-und-leistung\">LEIFIphysik\n– Work, energy and power<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Forms, conversion, work, power and\nconservation of energy · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/bildungsministerkonferenz/bildungsthemen/bildungsstandards.html\">KMK\n– Physics standards for intermediate school certificate (2024)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nOfficial index of 2024 physics competence standards · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M8-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M8-L7 – Measurement and calculation tasks for energy accounts<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M8 – Energy, work and power<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>You raise a toy cart onto a ramp, then measure its height and speed\nnear the bottom. The energy calculated from its height may exceed the\nmeasured kinetic energy. Is this a contradiction, or a question of\nboundaries and measurement quality?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical concepts and model<\/h2>\n<p>A sound <strong>energy account<\/strong> follows four steps: (1)\nSelect the system and its initial and final states. (2) Identify\nstored-energy terms and transfers. (3) Insert measured numbers with\nunits and uncertainties. (4) Interpret any difference without\nattributing it to a single cause without evidence.<\/p>\n<p>For a cart starting from rest, rolling down a vertical height\ndifference\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand reaching speed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>v<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe use the model<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">,<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mi>m<\/mi><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mi>m<\/mi><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_{\\mathrm{start}}=mgh,\\qquad E_{\\mathrm{kin,final}}=\\tfrac12mv^2,\\qquad E_{\\mathrm{residual}}=mgh-\\tfrac12mv^2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_{\\mathrm{residual}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis initially just an <strong>accounting residual<\/strong>. With wheels\nit can include rotational kinetic energy; internal energy changes,\nvibration, sound and measurement error can also contribute. Height is\nmeasured from the chosen bottom reference level. A negative residual is\nnot automatically “negative friction” but a reason to check the model\nand measurements.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Measurement uncertainty:<\/strong> Report values such as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.50<\/mn><mo>±<\/mo><mn>0.01<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=(0.50\\pm0.01)\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrather than numerous unjustified decimal places. A relative error in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas a roughly proportional effect on\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">mgh<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nbecause\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>v<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis squared, its relative error can have approximately twice the effect\non the kinetic-energy estimate. At lower-secondary level a justified\nrange is often more useful than a full statistical propagation\ncalculation.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Original diagram:<\/strong> Ramp measurement sketch with a\nthree-line accounting panel showing measurements, calculated joules and\nresidual.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_050_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Ramp with 0.80 m height, cart mass 0.50 kg and speed 3.0 m/s; residual between gravitational and kinetic energy.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Ramp with 0.80 m height, cart mass 0.50\nkg and speed 3.0 m/s; residual between gravitational and kinetic\nenergy.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Measured example with an explicitly unresolved energy\nresidual.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example or application<\/h2>\n<p>A cart has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=0.50\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ndrops by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.80<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=0.80\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand has a measured lower-point speed of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=3.0\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nApproximately,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.50<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>9.81<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.80<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.924<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_{\\mathrm{start}}=0.50\\cdot9.81\\cdot0.80=3.924\\,\\mathrm{J},<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n<math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">,<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.50<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3.0<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.25<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_{\\mathrm{kin,final}}=\\tfrac12\\cdot0.50\\cdot(3.0)^2=2.25\\,\\mathrm{J},<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n<math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.924<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2.25<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.674<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_{\\mathrm{residual}}=3.924-2.25=1.674\\,\\mathrm{J}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The residual is approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>42.7<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">42.7\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof initial energy. It is <strong>not necessarily all frictional\nwork<\/strong>, since wheel rotation and other sources must be\nconsidered. Report no more significant figures than the measurement\nquality supports.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe observation and experiment<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Safe measurement protocol:<\/strong> Use a short, secured toy\nramp and a harmless cart. Measure mass, height difference and speed\nusing timed positions or a suitable classroom light gate. Catch the cart\non a padded surface. Make a table of quantity, value, unit and estimated\nuncertainty. Change only one experimental condition at a time.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">List the four steps in a measurement-based energy account.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">mgh<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor 0.20 kg raised by 0.50 m, taking\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9.81<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g=9.81\\,\\mathrm{N/kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">If the starting account has 2.0 J and the measured kinetic energy is\n1.6 J, what is the residual?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Does the residual for a wheeled cart necessarily measure pure\nfrictional energy? Explain.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Select system/states, list accounts and transfers, calculate with\nunits and uncertainties, interpret the residual.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.20<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>9.81<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.50<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.981<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.20\\cdot9.81\\cdot0.50=0.981\\,\\mathrm{J}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.0<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1.6<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.0-1.6=0.4\\,\\mathrm{J}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No. Rotational energy, other transfers, measurement variation and\nincomplete modelling also matter.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Without additional measurements of wheel rotation, friction and\nuncertainty, the residual cannot be uniquely separated. A negative\nresult calls for careful diagnosis, not rejection of conservation of\nenergy. Subsequent lessons revisit electrical circuits and energy\ntransfer.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Looking ahead:<\/strong> The next module begins with <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M9&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M9-L1 – Revisiting charge, current\nand voltage<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/mechanik/arbeit-energie-und-leistung/grundwissen/energieerhaltung\">LEIFIphysik\n– Energy conservation<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Ideal conservation of mechanical energy and\nfrictional pathways · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/mechanik/arbeit-energie-und-leistung\">LEIFIphysik\n– Work, energy and power<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Forms, conversion, work, power and\nconservation of energy · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/rlp-online/c-faecher/physik/kompetenzen-und-standards\">Berlin-Brandenburg\nEducation Server – Physics competences<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Conservation, conversion\nand efficiency in competence standards · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 ·\nContent verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/rlp-online/c-faecher/physik/themen-und-inhalte\">Berlin-Brandenburg\nEducation Server – Physics curriculum topics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Mechanical energy and\nwork; energy transformation · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/bildungsministerkonferenz/bildungsthemen/bildungsstandards.html\">KMK\n– Physics standards for intermediate school certificate (2024)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nOfficial index of 2024 physics competence standards · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
